NAME
       chown - change the owner and group of files

SYNOPSIS
       chown [OPTION...] [OWNER][:[GROUP]] FILE...
       chown [OPTION...] --reference=RFILE FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       chown sets the user who owns each FILE, its group, or both. Only root
       may give a file to another user. A normal user who owns a file may use
       chown (or chgrp) to change its group to one of their own groups.

       The first argument says what to change:

       OWNER          change only the owner
       OWNER:GROUP    change the owner and the group
       OWNER:         change the owner, and set the group to the owner's
                      login group
       :GROUP         change only the group (the same as chgrp GROUP)

       Names or numbers (UIDs and GIDs) both work. An older form with a dot
       (OWNER.GROUP) is still accepted, but the colon is the standard.

       When root changes the owner of a program, its set-user-ID and
       set-group-ID bits are cleared for safety.

OPTIONS
       -R, --recursive
              Change the files and directories inside each directory too.

       -v, --verbose
              Report every file processed.

       -c, --changes
              Report only the files that really changed.

       -f, --silent, --quiet
              Do not print most error messages.

       -h, --no-dereference
              Change a symbolic link itself instead of the file it points to.

       --from=OWNER[:GROUP]
              Change only files whose current owner (and group) match.

       --reference=RFILE
              Use RFILE's owner and group.

EXAMPLES
       Give a file to student:

           chown student /home/student/report.txt

       Give a directory tree to the user and group apache:

           chown -R apache:apache /var/www/html/app

       Set the owner to student and the group to student's login group:

           chown student: /srv/project

       Change only the group:

           chown :devs /shared/devs

       Hand over everything that still belongs to a removed account (UID 1005):

           chown -R --from=1005 student /home/olduser

SEE ALSO
       chgrp(1), chmod(1), ls(1), find(1), id(1)
